752:  - replaces `$HOME` with an empty tmpfs (`~/.ssh`, `~/.aws`, `~/.gnupg`, `~/.config/nexus` are not visible, also when the workspace is `$HOME`) and binds back, read-only, only toolchain directories (`~/.cargo`, `~/.rustup`, `~/.nvm`, `~/.local/bin`, `~/.npm`, `~/.cache/pip`, `~/go/pkg`, `PATH` entries under `$HOME`). Extend the list with `NEXUS_SANDBOX_HOME_READONLY` (comma-separated paths, operator environment or `config.env` only). Cargo credential files, `/run/user/<uid>` and the docker socket are hidden. `nexus doctor` prints the effective mounts.
754:  - has **no network by default**. Opt in with `--sandbox-network` or `NEXUS_SANDBOX_NETWORK=1` in the operator environment; `--yes` does not enable it, and `--read-only` is always offline. In review mode the approval prompt states whether the sandbox has network access.
756:  - Docker (`--sandbox docker`): a digest-pinned image (`alpine:3.23@sha256:...`, constant `DOCKER_SANDBOX_IMAGE`; override with the operator setting `NEXUS_SANDBOX_DOCKER_IMAGE`, which must be `name@sha256:<64 hex>` or no container starts), run as your uid:gid with `--cap-drop ALL`, `--security-opt no-new-privileges`, `--pids-limit 512`, a read-only root and a 256 MB tmpfs `/tmp`, no network unless `--sandbox-network` / `NEXUS_SANDBOX_NETWORK=1`, the scrubbed environment only (`--env` per variable), and credential paths masked when the workspace is, contains or sits above `$HOME`. Commands that wrote outside the workspace and `/tmp`, relied on root, or used the network now fail in Docker mode.
757:  - **Limits:** under `--sandbox`, files outside `$HOME` stay readable (the host root is bound read-only), for example other users' home directories and world-readable system files. The sandbox does not protect against a command you approve while the network opt-in is on.
758:- **Command Environment Scrubbing**: `run_command` no longer inherits credentials from the Nexus process, with or without `--sandbox`. Variables set in `~/.config/nexus/config.env`, names that look like credentials (`*_API_KEY`, `*_TOKEN`, `*_SECRET`, `*_PASSWORD`, `AWS_*`, `GH_TOKEN`, `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`, ...) and proxy URLs with embedded credentials are removed. To pass a variable through deliberately, list its name in `NEXUS_COMMAND_ENV_PASSTHROUGH` (comma-separated, trailing `*` for a prefix; operator environment or `config.env` only, never a project `.env`). Commands that relied on `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`, `GH_TOKEN` and similar must now opt in. **Limit:** without `--sandbox` the command runs as your user: it can still read your files (`~/.config/nexus/config.env`, `~/.ssh`) and its parent processes' environment through `/proc/<pid>/environ`. Use `--sandbox` to hide host processes and `$HOME`.
760:- **In-Process Plugin Execution & Middleware Auditing**: Custom plugin handlers and middleware execute in-process with full user authority. Plugins are loaded only from documented directories: `~/.config/nexus/plugins/`, `~/.nexus/plugins/`, and trusted workspace directories (`.nexus/plugins/`, `plugins/`). The undocumented foreign directory `~/.config/opencode/plugins` has been removed (REQ-SEC-115), and the legacy worker-thread sandbox claim has been retired (`executeSandboxed` deleted, REQ-SEC-117). Any middleware rewriting of tool I/O (`preToolExecution` or `postToolExecution`) is cryptographically audited with before-and-after SHA-256 digests in both `~/.nexus/audit.log` (`kind: "middleware_rewrite"`) and the flight log (EXT-004, REQ-SEC-116).
783:- **Destructive Command Convenience Tripwire (REQ-SEC-108)**: Shell-words tokenization in TypeScript and Rust blocks obviously destructive commands (`rm -rf /`, `mkfs`, raw block device writes, fork bombs) without false positives on legitimate project commands (`rm -rf ./build`, `rm -rf /tmp/x`, `grep reboot`, `echo shutdown`, `chown -R me /srv`). This denylist is a **convenience tripwire** to catch obvious mistakes, **not a security boundary**. Known bypasses (such as `find / -delete`, `/bin/rm -rf /`, `rm -r -f /`, Python scripts) are documented non-goals that pass by design; the sandbox (`--sandbox`) and approval posture (`review`) form the security boundary.
